; ======================================================================
; pi4WifiKey.pi4 - ASSOCIATE, RUN THE FOUR-WAY HANDSHAKE IN THE HOST,
; INSTALL THE KEYS, AND SHOW THE LINK CARRYING ENCRYPTED TRAFFIC.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHY THE HOST HAS TO RUN THE HANDSHAKE, AND HOW THAT WAS SETTLED
; WITHOUT SPENDING A SINGLE BENCH RUN ON IT
; ======================================================================
; The Pico and the Uno R4 hand their radio an ASCII passphrase and the
; radio firmware does PBKDF2, the four-way handshake and the keying.
; That is not a property of the CYW43 family. It is a property of the
; FIRMWARE IMAGE, and the two images on this bench say so themselves -
; every Broadcom build carries the list of components it was compiled
; with, in plain text, inside the blob:
;
;   Firmware/CYW43439/wb43439A0-7.95.49.00.combined.bin   (the Pico's)
;     43439a0-roml/sdio-g-pool-p2p-IDSUP-IDAUTH-pktfilter-keepalive-
;     aoe-lpc-swdiv-srfast-fuart-btcx-noclminc-clm_min-fbt-mfp-SAE-
;     wowlpf-tko-nvd-btsdio            Version: 7.95.61 (abcd531 CY)
;
;   Firmware/CYW43455/brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin  (this one)
;     43455c0-roml/43455_sdio-pno-aoe-pktfilter-pktctx-wfds-mfp-
;     dfsradar-wowlpf-noclminc-clm_min-obss-obssdump-swdiv-gtkoe-
;     roamprof-txbf-ve-EXTSAE-dpp-sr-okc-bpd
;                                      Version: 7.45.265 (28bca26 CY)
;
; `idsup` is the IN-DONGLE SUPPLICANT, `idauth` the in-dongle
; authenticator. THE PICO'S IMAGE HAS BOTH AND THIS ONE HAS NEITHER,
; and where the Pico's says `sae` this one says `extsae` - EXTERNAL
; SAE, meaning the host does it.
;
; That is the whole explanation for the four refusals pi4WifiJoin.pi4
; measured on 2026-08-27 (`bsscfg:sup_wpa`, `bsscfg:sup_wpa2_eapver`
; and `bsscfg:sup_wpa_tmo` all -23 BCME_UNSUPPORTED, and
; WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK -2 BCME_BADARG at both documented struct sizes).
; Four measurements, one cause, and the cause was readable in a file
; already on this disk.
;
; IT IS ALSO WHAT EVERY RASPBERRY PI DOES. brcmfmac with this same
; image has no firmware supplicant either; wpa_supplicant in userspace
; runs the handshake over the data path and plumbs the finished keys
; down with `bsscfg:wsec_key`. This payload is that, minus the
; operating system.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT TO TYPE, IN THIS ORDER
; ======================================================================
;     pmf> screen off
;     pmf> load BRCMFW.BIN  1000000
;     pmf> load BRCMNV.TXT  1200000
;     pmf> load BRCMCLM.BLB 1300000
;     pmf> fill 1400000 0 800
;     pmf> load SETTINGS.TXT 1400000
;     pmf> deadman 15
;     pmf> map              <- it prints `stage a file at <stage>`. An
;                              image is the same bytes at any --load-addr.
;     pmf> run <stage>
;
; AND pi4WifiPmk.pi4 MUST HAVE RUN FIRST, until it says "all staged" or
; at least until the network you want is in its table. This payload
; does NOT derive a PMK: PBKDF2-SHA1 at 4096 iterations measures about
; ten seconds on this part with the MMU off, and the whole budget here
; is fifteen. The staged table lives at $01500000 and carries a
; fingerprint of SETTINGS.TXT so a stale one cannot be used silently.
;
; `screen off` IS IN THE LIST FOR A MEASURED REASON. On 2026-08-28,
; after a deadman reset, Anvil came back with the HDMI framebuffer
; enabled and the very next payload could not get an answer out of the
; mailbox: `MailboxClockRate(1)` returned zero and the run stopped at
; "the firmware would not report the EMMC clock" with the radio never
; touched. The clock query below therefore RETRIES and prints each
; attempt, so that if it happens again the log says whether it is a
; hard refusal or a race.
;
; ======================================================================
; NO CREDENTIAL IS PRINTED, DERIVED OR OTHERWISE
; ======================================================================
; The passphrase never enters this payload at all - it stops at
; pi4WifiPmk.pi4. The PMK is read from DRAM and handed to wpa2sup.pi4;
; the PTK, the TK and the GTK are computed, installed and wiped. What
; is printed is lengths, verdicts, MAC addresses, an RSSI and a
; channel. Nothing here is a key or a function of one.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT SUCCESS LOOKS LIKE, IN ORDER
; ======================================================================
;   1. the staged table is valid and holds the network we will join
;   2. `wpaie` is accepted, and `assoc_req_ies` reads back the SAME
;      bytes - which is what lets message 2 repeat them exactly
;   3. association: AUTH 0 and LINK flags bit 0
;   4. EAPOL message 1 arrives as a data frame
;   5. message 2 goes out; message 3 arrives AND ITS MIC VERIFIES -
;      that is the moment the access point proves it holds the same
;      PMK, and it is the milestone
;   6. the PTK and the GTK go into the firmware
;   7. message 4 goes out
;   8. encrypted traffic arrives and decodes as valid Ethernet
; ======================================================================

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43_rx_glom.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/entropy.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sha1.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/hmacsha1.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/aes.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/keywrap.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Network/net.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/wpa2sup.pi4"

; Where Anvil's `load` put each file. pi4WifiScan.pi4's addresses,
; lengths and CRCs, measured on the board by reading each file back off
; the stick, and unchanged here.
#FW_ADDR = $01000000
#FW_LEN  = 609309

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TWO 4 KB WINDOWS OF THE FIRMWARE, NOT ALL 609,309 BYTES, AND THE
;  REASON IS A MEASUREMENT.
;
;  pi4WifiJoin.pi4 CRCs the whole image and on 2026-08-28 this payload
;  printed
;
;      [t+5849 ms] blobs checked
;
;  FIVE POINT EIGHT SECONDS of a fifteen-second budget, spent before the
;  radio is touched, on a check of bytes that are about to be verified
;  anyway. The MMU is off, so the CRC reads 609 KB of uncached Device
;  memory a byte at a time.
;
;  WHAT THE CHECK IS ACTUALLY FOR: "did somebody forget the `load`, or
;  load the wrong file". The head window catches a missing or truncated
;  load and the wrong image; the tail window catches a SHORT one, which
;  the head alone would not. Both are computed from
;  Firmware/CYW43455/brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin.
;
;  AND THE FULL INTEGRITY CHECK STILL HAPPENS, just not here:
;  Cyw43UploadFirmware reads every byte back out of the radio's RAM and
;  compares it with the copy in DRAM (Cyw43VerifyMem), so a corrupt
;  middle fails the upload with an address. Doing it twice cost more
;  than a third of the budget.
;
;  THE SMALL BLOBS ARE STILL CHECKED WHOLE. 2,074 and 2,676 bytes
;  together are about 45 ms; there is nothing to buy by trimming them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#FW_WINDOW   = 4096
#FW_CRC_HEAD = $A61DFDB1
#FW_CRC_TAIL = $9E75B809

#NV_ADDR = $01200000
#NV_LEN  = 2074
#NV_CRC  = $2BDBDDF6

#CLM_ADDR = $01300000
#CLM_LEN  = 2676
#CLM_CRC  = $346D4B26

#SET_ADDR = $01400000
#SET_WIN  = 8192

#NV_WORK      = $01210000
#NV_WORK_MAX  = 4096
#SCRATCH      = $01220000
#SCRATCH_LEN  = 2048

; The staged PMK table. pi4WifiPmk.pi4 owns the layout; this is the
; reader's copy of it and the two must agree.
#PMKS_ADDR      = $01500000
#PMKS_MAGIC     = $504D4B31
#PMKS_HDR       = 16
#PMKS_OFF_COUNT = 4
#PMKS_OFF_FP    = 8
#PMKS_ENTRY     = 80
#PMKS_OFF_SLOT  = 0
#PMKS_OFF_SLEN  = 4
#PMKS_OFF_SSID  = 8
#PMKS_OFF_PMK   = 40
#PMKS_MAX       = 8

; Mailbox clock id 1 = EMMC. NOT 12, which is EMMC2's.
#CLOCK_ID_EMMC = 1
#CLOCK_TRIES   = 8
#CLOCK_GAP_MS  = 25

; The join's polling slice, and the caller's own ceiling on it. The
; library owns the real guards; this is the belt to their braces.
#JOIN_SLICE_MS   = 20
#JOIN_MAX_SLICES = 400

;  THE LADDER'S WALL CLOCK. Four staged networks at one attempt each
;  measured 13,430 ms on 2026-08-28 and left nothing for the handshake
;  - and two of those four are the truck's and are not on the air at
;  this bench. 9,500 ms is what the ladder may spend; everything after
;  it belongs to the four-way handshake, which needs about 300 ms, and
;  to the DHCP exchange, which is gated separately.
#JOIN_LADDER_BY_MS = 9500

; How long to wait for each EAPOL message. An authenticator retries
; message 1 about once a second, four times; 1200 ms covers the first
; and one retry without eating the budget. Message 3 follows message 2
; within milliseconds, so its window is shorter.
#EAPOL_M1_MS = 2600
#EAPOL_M3_MS = 800

; How long to listen for encrypted traffic once the keys are in. Any
; real LAN broadcasts ARP and multicast constantly, so this does not
; need to be long to prove the link decrypts - and every millisecond
; here is one the DHCP exchange below does not have.
#TRAFFIC_MS = 150

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DHCP, AND WHY THERE IS NO STATIC ADDRESS IN THIS FILE.
;
;  A ping needs an address, a netmask and somewhere to send it. This
;  bench's SETTINGS.TXT holds four Wi-Fi credentials and NOTHING ELSE -
;  the eight keys the parser reports are four SSIDs and four
;  passphrases, with no net.address, net.netmask or net.gateway among
;  them. So there is nothing to read, and the only alternatives are to
;  ask the network or to invent a number.
;
;  INVENTING ONE IS NOT AN OPTION ON SOMEBODY'S HOME LAN. A guessed
;  192.168.1.50 that happens to be in use takes another machine off the
;  network, and it does it silently. So: DHCP, built by hand here
;  rather than through net.pi4, whose header says plainly that it has
;  no DHCP and that the omission is deliberate (net.pi4:266). Four
;  frames, no library, and it returns the mask and the router as well
;  as the address.
;
;  THE EXCHANGE IS ALSO THE PROOF. A DHCP OFFER comes back over the
;  air CCMP-encrypted with the keys this payload just installed, so
;  reaching an ACK proves the link both ways at the IP layer before a
;  single ICMP packet is sent.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DHCP_FRAME_LEN = 342          ; 14 eth + 20 ip + 8 udp + 300 bootp
#DHCP_WAIT_MS   = 1800
#DHCP_ACK_MS    = 1200
#PING_WAIT_MS   = 600
#ARP_WAIT_MS    = 800
#PING_COUNT     = 3
;  The wall-clock gate on the whole DHCP-and-ping stage. Eleven seconds
;  of fifteen, which leaves room for a DISCOVER, an OFFER, a REQUEST,
;  an ACK, an ARP and two echoes if every one of them is prompt, and
;  refuses to start if the association has already eaten the budget.
#PING_START_BY_MS = 12000

; THE SPIN BOUND IS entropy.pi4's OWN #ENTROPY_SPINS and this file does
; not define a second one. It tried to, with a smaller number, and the
; compiler refused: "already defined as 4000000 and this redefines it as
; 200000 ... that CompilerIf may be in an included library you never
; opened." That refusal is the project's whole philosophy working - a
; quieter compiler would have let the smaller bound win inside the
; library as well as here.

Global g_hz.i = 0
Global startMs.i = 0
Global Dim ourMac.a[6]
Global Dim apMac.a[6]
Global Dim snonce.a[32]
; The largest RSN element this file will carry. DECLARED HERE AND NOT
; beside the element it bounds, because it sizes theIe below and a
; constant used as an array size has to exist first - the compiler
; refuses rather than assuming, which is how this was found.
#RSN_IE_MAX = 64

Global Dim thePmk.a[32]
; The RSN element as the firmware ACTUALLY TRANSMITTED it, read back
; from `assoc_req_ies`. This is what message 2 repeats.
Global Dim theIe.a[#RSN_IE_MAX]
Global theIeLen.i = 0
Global Dim dhcpXid.a[4]
Global Dim peerMac.a[6]
; The DHCP server's hardware address, taken from the source field
; of its own OFFER or ACK. See the ARP seeding below.
Global Dim srvMac.a[6]
Global srvMacOk.i = 0
; What the network says about a file server, if anything.
Global dhcpNext.i = 0        ; BOOTP siaddr
Global dhcpTftpIp.i = 0      ; DHCP option 150, first entry
Global Dim dhcpTftpName.a[64]
Global dhcpTftpNameLen.i = 0
Global dhcpIp.i   = 0
Global dhcpMask.i = 0
Global dhcpGw.i   = 0
Global dhcpSrv.i  = 0

Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Line(*name, v.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec(v)
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

Procedure HexLine(*name, v.i, digits.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  $")
  PutHexN(v, digits)
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

; An SSID is 32 arbitrary bytes chosen by whoever runs the access point,
; so everything outside printable ASCII is replaced: a name carrying
; escape sequences must not be able to rewrite the lines above it in a
; log destined for a report.
Procedure PrintSsid(p.i, n.i)
  Define j.i
  If n <= 0
    UartWriteStr("<hidden>")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If n > 32
    n = 32
  EndIf
  For j = 0 To n - 1
    If PeekA(p + j) >= 32 And PeekA(p + j) <= 126
      UartWrite(PeekA(p + j))
    Else
      UartWrite(46)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure PrintBssid(p.i)
  Define j.i
  For j = 0 To 5
    PutHexN(PeekA(p + j) & $FF, 2)
    If j < 5
      UartWrite(58)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure.i NowMs()
  If g_hz < 1000
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn Ticks() / (g_hz / 1000)
EndProcedure

Procedure Stamp(*what)
  UartWriteStr("  [t+")
  PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms] ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Crc32(addr.i, len.i)
  Define crc.i
  Define i.i
  Define b.i
  Define k.i
  crc = $FFFFFFFF
  For i = 0 To len - 1
    crc = crc ! PeekA(addr + i)
    For k = 0 To 7
      b = crc & 1
      crc = (crc >> 1) & $7FFFFFFF
      If b <> 0
        crc = crc ! $EDB88320
      EndIf
    Next
  Next
  ProcedureReturn (crc ! $FFFFFFFF) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Rd32(p.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekA(p) | (PeekA(p + 1) << 8) | (PeekA(p + 2) << 16) | (PeekA(p + 3) << 24)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Fnv1a(p.i, n.i)
  Define h.i
  Define i.i
  h = $811C9DC5
  For i = 0 To n - 1
    h = (h ! PeekA(p + i)) & $FFFFFFFF
    h = (h * 16777619) & $FFFFFFFF
  Next
  ProcedureReturn h
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE RSN INFORMATION ELEMENT WE OFFER, AND WHY IT IS A CONSTANT.
;
;      30 14                        id 48, length 20
;      01 00                        RSN version 1
;      00 0F AC 04                  group cipher   CCMP
;      01 00  00 0F AC 04           one pairwise   CCMP
;      01 00  00 0F AC 02           one AKM        PSK
;      00 00                        RSN capabilities: none
;
;  THESE EXACT BYTES GO OUT TWICE - into the association request
;  through the `wpaie` iovar, and into EAPOL message 2 - and the
;  authenticator compares the two. That is IEEE 802.11 12.7.6.3, and
;  hostapd deauthenticates on a mismatch. Having the host choose them
;  is the only way to be sure the two copies agree; a firmware-
;  synthesised element would have to be guessed at, and a guess one
;  capability bit out fails the handshake with no diagnosis.
;
;  THE CAPABILITIES FIELD IS $000C AND THAT NUMBER CAME OFF THE BOARD.
;  Measured 2026-08-28: we sent $0000 through `wpaie` and the firmware
;  transmitted $000C. Every other byte was identical, so the firmware
;  DOES carry the element it is given and then overwrites this one
;  field with its own capability - bits 2 and 3 are the PTKSA Replay
;  Counter field, and 3 means sixteen counters per PTKSA.
;
;  MESSAGE 2 CARRIED OUR $0000 AND THE ACCESS POINT DROPPED IT. IEEE
;  802.11 12.7.6.3 has the authenticator verify that message 2's RSNE
;  is identical to the association request's; hostapd logs "RSN IE in
;  msg 2/4 does not match with IE in association request" and says
;  nothing on the air. Message 3 simply never arrives, which reads
;  exactly like a wrong passphrase and is not one.
;
;  MATCHING IT HERE IS BELT; THE BRACES ARE THE READ-BACK. Section 7b
;  asks the firmware what the request actually carried and hands THOSE
;  bytes to the supplicant, so this constant only has to be close
;  enough to make the association itself sensible.
;
;  NO MANAGEMENT FRAME PROTECTION - bit 6 (MFPC) and bit 7 (MFPR) are
;  both clear, and that is a real limitation: this driver has no
;  BIP-CMAC-128 and `bsscfg:mfp` is one of the iovars this firmware
;  refuses. An access point that REQUIRES MFP will answer 802.11 status
;  43 AKMP_NOT_VALID - which is exactly what ajtaji_5G already answers.
;
;  NO PMKID LIST. Its absence is what the end of the element says.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#RSN_IE_LEN = 22

DataSection
  theRsnIe:
    Data.b $30, $14, $01, $00
    Data.b $00, $0F, $AC, $04
    Data.b $01, $00, $00, $0F, $AC, $04
    Data.b $01, $00, $00, $0F, $AC, $02
    Data.b $0C, $00
EndDataSection

; ======================================================================
;  A MINIMAL DHCP CLIENT - DISCOVER, OFFER, REQUEST, ACK.
;
;  Everything is big-endian on the wire and every field is written a
;  byte at a time. That is not fussiness: the MMU is off, so every
;  access is Device memory and an unaligned wide store faults - the
;  same rule that made cyw43_PutLE32 four PokeB calls.
; ======================================================================
Procedure PutBE16(p.i, v.i)
  PokeB(p, (v >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(p + 1, v & $FF)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutBE32(p.i, v.i)
  PokeB(p, (v >> 24) & $FF)
  PokeB(p + 1, (v >> 16) & $FF)
  PokeB(p + 2, (v >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(p + 3, v & $FF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i GetBE16(p.i)
  ProcedureReturn (PeekA(p) << 8) | PeekA(p + 1)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i GetBE32(p.i)
  ProcedureReturn (PeekA(p) << 24) | (PeekA(p + 1) << 16) | (PeekA(p + 2) << 8) | PeekA(p + 3)
EndProcedure

Procedure PrintIp(v.i)
  PrintDec((v >> 24) & $FF)
  UartWrite(46)
  PrintDec((v >> 16) & $FF)
  UartWrite(46)
  PrintDec((v >> 8) & $FF)
  UartWrite(46)
  PrintDec(v & $FF)
EndProcedure

; The IPv4 header checksum: the ones-complement sum of the ten 16-bit
; words, folded and complemented. The checksum field must read zero
; while it is computed, which the zeroed buffer already guarantees.
Procedure.i IpChecksum(ip.i)
  Define s.i
  Define i.i
  s = 0
  For i = 0 To 9
    s = s + GetBE16(ip + i * 2)
  Next
  While (s >> 16) <> 0
    s = (s & $FFFF) + (s >> 16)
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn (~s) & $FFFF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  BuildDhcp - a complete DISCOVER (kind 1) or REQUEST (kind 3) in the
;  radio's transmit buffer. Returns the frame length.
;
;  THE BROADCAST FLAG IS SET, and it is the one bit that decides
;  whether this works at all: with no address configured we cannot
;  accept a unicast IP datagram, so the server is asked to broadcast
;  its reply instead. RFC 2131 section 4.1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i BuildDhcp(kind.i, reqIp.i, srvIp.i)
  Define p.i
  Define ip.i
  Define u.i
  Define b.i
  Define o.i
  Define i.i

  p = Cyw43DataPtr()
  For i = 0 To #DHCP_FRAME_LEN - 1
    PokeB(p + i, 0)
  Next

  For i = 0 To 5
    PokeB(p + i, $FF)
    PokeB(p + 6 + i, ourMac[i])
  Next
  PokeB(p + 12, $08)
  PokeB(p + 13, $00)

  ip = p + 14
  PokeB(ip + 0, $45)
  PutBE16(ip + 2, #DHCP_FRAME_LEN - 14)
  PutBE16(ip + 4, $B00B)
  PokeB(ip + 8, 64)
  PokeB(ip + 9, 17)
  PutBE32(ip + 16, $FFFFFFFF)
  PutBE16(ip + 10, IpChecksum(ip))

  u = p + 34
  PutBE16(u + 0, 68)
  PutBE16(u + 2, 67)
  PutBE16(u + 4, #DHCP_FRAME_LEN - 34)
  ; The UDP checksum stays zero, which IPv4 permits and which every
  ; DHCP server accepts - a client with no address of its own cannot
  ; build the pseudo-header it would be computed over.

  b = p + 42
  PokeB(b + 0, 1)              ; BOOTREQUEST
  PokeB(b + 1, 1)              ; Ethernet
  PokeB(b + 2, 6)              ; six-byte hardware address
  For i = 0 To 3
    PokeB(b + 4 + i, dhcpXid[i])
  Next
  PutBE16(b + 10, $8000)       ; BROADCAST. See the header.
  For i = 0 To 5
    PokeB(b + 28 + i, ourMac[i])
  Next
  PokeB(b + 236, 99)
  PokeB(b + 237, 130)
  PokeB(b + 238, 83)
  PokeB(b + 239, 99)

  o = b + 240
  PokeB(o, 53)
  PokeB(o + 1, 1)
  PokeB(o + 2, kind)
  o = o + 3
  If kind = 3
    PokeB(o, 50)
    PokeB(o + 1, 4)
    PutBE32(o + 2, reqIp)
    o = o + 6
    PokeB(o, 54)
    PokeB(o + 1, 4)
    PutBE32(o + 2, srvIp)
    o = o + 6
  EndIf
  PokeB(o, 55)                 ; parameter request list
  PokeB(o + 1, 5)
  PokeB(o + 2, 1)              ; subnet mask
  PokeB(o + 3, 3)              ; router
  PokeB(o + 4, 6)              ; domain name servers
  PokeB(o + 5, 66)             ; TFTP server NAME
  PokeB(o + 6, 150)            ; TFTP server ADDRESS list
  o = o + 7
  PokeB(o, 255)

  ProcedureReturn #DHCP_FRAME_LEN
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ParseDhcp - is this frame a DHCP reply to OUR transaction, and if so
;  what kind? Returns the option 53 message type, or 0.
;
;  EVERY BOUND IS CHECKED AGAINST THE LENGTH THE CALLER GAVE, because
;  this is the first thing in this payload to walk bytes that came off
;  the air from a machine nobody here controls. The IHL is honoured
;  rather than assumed to be five: a server that puts options in the IP
;  header would otherwise move every field and the parse would read the
;  wrong ones without noticing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ParseDhcp(f.i, n.i)
  Define b.i
  Define o.i
  Define t.i
  Define l.i
  Define hdr.i
  Define kind.i

  If n < 42 + 240
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PeekA(f + 12) <> $08 Or PeekA(f + 13) <> $00
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (PeekA(f + 14) >> 4) <> 4
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  hdr = (PeekA(f + 14) & $F) * 4
  If hdr < 20 Or (14 + hdr + 8 + 240) > n
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PeekA(f + 14 + 9) <> 17
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If GetBE16(f + 14 + hdr) <> 67 Or GetBE16(f + 14 + hdr + 2) <> 68
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  b = f + 14 + hdr + 8
  If PeekA(b) <> 2
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  For l = 0 To 3
    If PeekA(b + 4 + l) <> dhcpXid[l]
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  Next
  If PeekA(b + 236) <> 99 Or PeekA(b + 237) <> 130 Or PeekA(b + 238) <> 83 Or PeekA(b + 239) <> 99
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  kind = 0
  o = b + 240
  While o < (f + n)
    t = PeekA(o)
    If t = 255
      Break
    EndIf
    If t = 0
      o = o + 1
      Continue
    EndIf
    If (o + 2) > (f + n)
      Break
    EndIf
    l = PeekA(o + 1)
    If (o + 2 + l) > (f + n)
      Break
    EndIf
    If t = 53 And l = 1
      kind = PeekA(o + 2)
    EndIf
    If t = 1 And l = 4
      dhcpMask = GetBE32(o + 2)
    EndIf
    If t = 3 And l >= 4
      dhcpGw = GetBE32(o + 2)
    EndIf
    If t = 54 And l = 4
      dhcpSrv = GetBE32(o + 2)
    EndIf
    ; OPTION 66 IS A NAME AND OPTION 150 IS A LIST OF ADDRESSES. Both
    ; are optional and most home routers send neither; the point of
    ; asking is to be able to say which.
    If t = 66 And l >= 1
      dhcpTftpNameLen = l
      If dhcpTftpNameLen > 63
        dhcpTftpNameLen = 63
      EndIf
      For hdr = 0 To dhcpTftpNameLen - 1
        dhcpTftpName[hdr] = PeekA(o + 2 + hdr)
      Next
    EndIf
    If t = 150 And l >= 4
      dhcpTftpIp = GetBE32(o + 2)
    EndIf
    o = o + 2 + l
  Wend

  If kind <> 0
    dhcpIp = GetBE32(b + 16)
    ; siaddr - "the address of the next server to use in bootstrap".
    ; Zero on almost every home router, which is itself the answer.
    dhcpNext = GetBE32(b + 20)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn kind
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Send whatever wpa2sup has staged, through the radio's data path.
;  Returns the transport's verdict.
;
;  BUILD IN PLACE IS NOT AN OPTION HERE and the copy is the price of
;  wpa2sup.pi4 owning no transport. It is at most 135 bytes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SendSupFrame()
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define src.i
  Define dst.i
  n = Wpa2SupTxLen()
  If n < 1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  src = Wpa2SupTxPtr()
  dst = Cyw43DataPtr()
  For i = 0 To n - 1
    PokeB(dst + i, PeekA(src + i))
  Next
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43Send(n)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Push whatever net.pi4 staged in its output buffer out of the radio.
;
;  ONE COPY PER FRAME, and it is the price of net.pi4 performing no I/O
;  by design - the same price the Ethernet path pays in the other
;  direction. At 60 bytes for an ARP and 74 for an echo request it is
;  not a price worth engineering away.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SendNetOut()
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define src.i
  Define dst.i
  n = NetOutLen()
  If n < 1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  src = NetOutBuf()
  dst = Cyw43DataPtr()
  For i = 0 To n - 1
    PokeB(dst + i, PeekA(src + i))
  Next
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43Send(n)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define ok.i
  Define r.i
  Define i.i
  Define j.i
  Define n.i
  Define p.i
  Define hz.i
  Define crc.i
  Define setLen.i
  Define fp.i
  Define count.i
  Define ent.i
  Define chosen.i
  Define ssid.i
  Define ssidLen.i
  Define slices.i
  Define verdict.i
  Define tries.i
  Define waitMs.i
  Define got.i
  Define code.i
  Define m1.i
  Define m3.i
  Define keyed.i
  Define frames.i
  Define t0.i
  Define k.i

  If TimerInit() <> 0
    g_hz = TickHz()
  EndIf
  startMs = NowMs()

  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("=== pi4WifiKey - host four-way handshake ===")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 1 - the blobs
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- the blobs, as loaded --")
  PrintNl()
  crc = Crc32(#FW_ADDR, #FW_WINDOW)
  HexLine("firmware head 4K ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #FW_CRC_HEAD
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - type:  load BRCMFW.BIN 1000000")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  crc = Crc32(#FW_ADDR + #FW_LEN - #FW_WINDOW, #FW_WINDOW)
  HexLine("firmware tail 4K ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #FW_CRC_TAIL
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG OR SHORT - type:  load BRCMFW.BIN 1000000")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  crc = Crc32(#NV_ADDR, #NV_LEN)
  HexLine("nvram crc32      ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #NV_CRC
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - type:  load BRCMNV.TXT 1200000")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  crc = Crc32(#CLM_ADDR, #CLM_LEN)
  HexLine("clm crc32        ", crc, 8)
  If crc <> #CLM_CRC
    UartWriteStr("  WRONG - type:  load BRCMCLM.BLB 1300000")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Stamp("blobs checked")

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 2 - THE STAGED PMK TABLE, AND ITS FINGERPRINT
  ;
  ; The fingerprint is over SETTINGS.TXT as it sits in DRAM right now.
  ; If it does not match the one pi4WifiPmk.pi4 recorded, the staged
  ; keys were derived from a different file and using them would fail
  ; message 3's MIC with no visible cause. REFUSED, not risked.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- the staged keys --")
  PrintNl()

  setLen = -1
  For i = 0 To #SET_WIN - 1
    If PeekA(#SET_ADDR + i) = 0
      setLen = i
      Break
    EndIf
  Next
  If setLen < 1
    UartWriteStr("  SETTINGS.TXT is not in DRAM. Type:")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    fill 1400000 0 800")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    load SETTINGS.TXT 1400000")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  fp = Fnv1a(#SET_ADDR, setLen)
  Line("settings bytes   ", setLen)
  HexLine("settings fingerprint", fp, 8)

  If Rd32(#PMKS_ADDR) <> #PMKS_MAGIC
    UartWriteStr("  NOTHING IS STAGED at $01500000. Run pi4WifiPmk.pi4")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  first - it derives the PMKs, which costs about ten")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  seconds each and cannot be done inside this payload's")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  fifteen-second budget.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If Rd32(#PMKS_ADDR + #PMKS_OFF_FP) <> fp
    UartWriteStr("  THE STAGED TABLE WAS BUILT FROM A DIFFERENT SETTINGS.TXT")
    PrintNl()
    HexLine("  it recorded    ", Rd32(#PMKS_ADDR + #PMKS_OFF_FP), 8)
    UartWriteStr("  Run pi4WifiPmk.pi4 again.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  count = Rd32(#PMKS_ADDR + #PMKS_OFF_COUNT)
  If count < 1 Or count > #PMKS_MAX
    Line("  staged count is not sane", count)
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Line("staged networks  ", count)
  For i = 0 To count - 1
    ent = #PMKS_ADDR + #PMKS_HDR + i * #PMKS_ENTRY
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintDec(i)
    UartWriteStr("  slot ")
    PrintDec(Rd32(ent + #PMKS_OFF_SLOT))
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintSsid(ent + #PMKS_OFF_SSID, Rd32(ent + #PMKS_OFF_SLEN))
    PrintNl()
  Next
  UartDrain()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 3 - the bus. THE CLOCK QUERY RETRIES, and the reason is a bench
  ;     measurement rather than caution: on 2026-08-28 the very same
  ;     call returned zero on the first attempt after a deadman reset
  ;     with Anvil's HDMI framebuffer enabled, and the run stopped
  ;     there with the radio never touched.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- bus --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = MailboxInit()
  Line("mailbox init (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  hz = 0
  tries = 0
  While tries < #CLOCK_TRIES And hz <= 0
    hz = MailboxClockRate(#CLOCK_ID_EMMC)
    UartWriteStr("  EMMC clock attempt ")
    PrintDec(tries + 1)
    UartWriteStr("  -> ")
    PrintDec(hz)
    UartWriteStr(" Hz")
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
    If hz <= 0
      delay(#CLOCK_GAP_MS)
    EndIf
    tries = tries + 1
  Wend
  If hz <= 0
    UartWriteStr("  THE FIRMWARE WOULD NOT REPORT THE EMMC CLOCK in ")
    PrintDec(#CLOCK_TRIES)
    UartWriteStr(" tries.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  Type `screen off` at the prompt and run again - Anvil's")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  framebuffer is the one thing known to have changed the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  day this first happened. sdio.pi4 will not guess a base")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  clock and neither will this file.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  SdioSetBaseClockKhz(hz / 1000)

  ok = SdioAssertWlOn()
  Line("WL_ON asserted (want 1)", ok)
  ok = SdioInit()
  Line("SdioInit (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ok = SdioEnableFunction(1)
  Line("enable F1 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 4 - the chip and the firmware
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- chip and firmware --")
  PrintNl()

  Cyw43SetIo(@SdioCmd52Read, @SdioCmd52Write, @SdioCmd53Read, @SdioCmd53Write)
  Cyw43SetBlockIo(@SdioCmd53ReadBlocks, @SdioCmd53WriteBlocks)

  ok = Cyw43Attach()
  Line("Cyw43Attach (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  HexLine("chip id          ", Cyw43ChipId(), 4)
  Cyw43SetBlockMode(1)

  ok = Cyw43PrepareDownload()
  Line("Cyw43PrepareDownload (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ok = Cyw43UploadFirmware(#FW_ADDR, #FW_LEN, #SCRATCH, #SCRATCH_LEN)
  Line("Cyw43UploadFirmware (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ok = Cyw43UploadNvram(#NV_ADDR, #NV_LEN, #NV_WORK, #NV_WORK_MAX, #SCRATCH, #SCRATCH_LEN)
  Line("Cyw43UploadNvram (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ok = Cyw43Start()
  Line("Cyw43Start (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ok = Cyw43HtClock()
  Line("Cyw43HtClock (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Cyw43AnnounceHost()
  ok = SdioEnableFunction(2)
  Line("enable F2 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Stamp("firmware up")

  r = Cyw43WifiUp(#CLM_ADDR, #CLM_LEN, #CYW43_EVMASK_JOIN, 2000)
  Line("Cyw43WifiUp (want 0)", r)
  If r <> 0
    Line("  ioctl status   ", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Stamp("radio up")

  r = Cyw43MacAddress(@ourMac[0], 2000)
  Line("cur_etheraddr rc ", r)
  If r <> 0
    UartWriteStr("  WITHOUT OUR OWN MAC THE PTK CANNOT BE DERIVED - it is")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  half of the Min/Max pair the derivation sorts.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  UartWriteStr("  our MAC          ")
  PrintBssid(@ourMac[0])
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5 - THE SNonce, FROM THE HARDWARE RANDOM NUMBER GENERATOR.
  ;
  ; A predictable SNonce makes every session key on this board
  ; derivable by anyone who captured the handshake, and it fails
  ; SILENTLY - the link comes up perfectly. wpa2sup.pi4 refuses to
  ; derive a PTK without one for exactly that reason.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- the nonce --")
  PrintNl()
  r = EntropyBegin()
  Line("EntropyBegin (want 0)", r)
  If r = 0
    r = EntropyWarmupWait(#ENTROPY_SPINS)
    Line("EntropyWarmupWait   ", r)
  EndIf
  n = EntropyBytes(@snonce[0], 32, #ENTROPY_SPINS)
  Line("EntropyBytes (want 32)", n)
  If n <> 32
    UartWriteStr("  NO ENTROPY, NO HANDSHAKE. A zero SNonce would associate")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  and key perfectly and would be a real weakness, so this")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  stops instead. See RaspberryPi4/Lib/entropy.pi4.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ; Not one byte of it is printed. Its ONLY published property is that
  ; it is not all zeros, which is the failure this checks for.
  ok = 0
  For i = 0 To 31
    If snonce[i] <> 0
      ok = 1
    EndIf
  Next
  Line("nonce is non-zero (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 6 - THE SECURITY BLOCK, WITHOUT THE SUPPLICANT KNOBS.
  ;
  ; Items 4, 5 and 6 (`bsscfg:sup_wpa`, `sup_wpa2_eapver`,
  ; `sup_wpa_tmo`) and item 7 (`bsscfg:mfp`) are all refused by this
  ; firmware and all four are OMITTED rather than sent-and-tolerated.
  ; Sending an ioctl in the certain knowledge that it will fail, and
  ; then ignoring the failure, teaches a reader that failures here do
  ; not matter - and the next real one will be ignored too.
  ;
  ; WHAT REMAINS IS WHAT THE ASSOCIATION REQUEST IS BUILT FROM:
  ;   0  ampdu_ba_wsize = 8
  ;   1  WLC_SET_INFRA = 1
  ;   2  bsscfg:auth = 0        802.11 OPEN SYSTEM
  ;   3  bsscfg:wsec = AES
  ;   8  bsscfg:wpa_auth = WPA2_PSK      LAST, as brcmfmac does
  ; plus the `wpaie` iovar, which is this payload's addition and is the
  ; thing that makes message 2 possible.
  ;
  ; NO WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK. There is nothing in this firmware to run a
  ; supplicant, so there is nothing to give a key to. The PMK stays in
  ; the host.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- security --")
  PrintNl()

  ; THE RSN ELEMENT GOES FIRST, which is brcmf_cfg80211_connect's order:
  ; `wpaie` is set at cfg80211.c:2409, above set_wpa_version,
  ; set_auth_type, set_wsec_mode and set_key_mgmt. It very likely does
  ; not matter - but the driver that works with this firmware every day
  ; does it this way, and matching it costs nothing.
  r = Cyw43SetWpaIe(?theRsnIe, #RSN_IE_LEN, 2000)
  Line("wpaie set (want 0)", r)
  If r <> 0
    Line("  ioctl status   ", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
    UartWriteStr("  WITHOUT `wpaie` THE HOST CANNOT KNOW WHAT THE ASSOCIATION")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  REQUEST CARRIED, and message 2 has to repeat it exactly.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = 1
  For i = 0 To 3
    r = Cyw43SecItem(i, 2000)
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    PrintDec(i)
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43SecItemName(i))
    UartWriteStr("   rc ")
    PrintDec(r)
    PrintNl()
    If r <> 0
      ok = 0
    EndIf
  Next
  r = Cyw43SecItem(8, 2000)
  UartWriteStr("  8  ")
  UartWriteStr(Cyw43SecItemName(8))
  UartWriteStr("   rc ")
  PrintDec(r)
  PrintNl()
  If r <> 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  UartDrain()
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  A SECURITY ITEM WAS REFUSED - the association request")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  would carry the wrong RSN information element.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  Stamp("security set")

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 7 - THE JOIN. Staged networks in order, first one that associates.
  ;
  ; NO SCAN, AND THE SAVING IS THE REASON. pi4WifiJoin.pi4's escan is
  ; five seconds of a fifteen-second budget and everything it produces
  ; - band, RSSI, BSSID - can be read back from the firmware AFTER
  ; association, which is where this file reads it. The one thing lost
  ; is the ability to prefer 5 GHz on measured signal strength before
  ; committing, and on this bench that preference is moot: ajtaji_5G
  ; answers 802.11 status 43 AKMP_NOT_VALID because it wants WPA3/SAE,
  ; which is recorded at length in pi4WifiJoin.pi4's finding 2.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- join --")
  PrintNl()

  ; NOKEY, not SECURED: the firmware will never emit a PSK_SUP event
  ; because it has no supplicant, so the accumulator must not wait for
  ; one. The KEYING happens below, in this payload, and its proof is
  ; message 3's MIC rather than an event.
  Cyw43SetJoinExpectation(#CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  DISASSOCIATE BEFORE THE FIRST ATTEMPT, WHICH cyw43.pi4 SAYS NOT TO
  ;  DO, AND HERE IS WHY IT IS RIGHT ANYWAY.
  ;
  ;  Cyw43Disassoc's header says "DO NOT SEND IT BEFORE A FIRST JOIN -
  ;  neither reference driver does". True for a DRIVER, whose first
  ;  join follows a cold boot. This is an INSTRUMENT, and its first
  ;  join follows the last run of itself. Measured 2026-08-28, the run
  ;  straight after one whose handshake failed at message 2:
  ;
  ;      [t+5067 ms] event 3 AUTH status 2 reason 0 state $0804
  ;
  ;  Status 2 is TIMEOUT: the access point was still holding a station
  ;  that had authenticated, associated and then vanished mid-
  ;  handshake. The twin measured what a clean teardown buys - attempt
  ;  one going from 4.3% to 75.3% over 144 reset-to-reset cycles, with
  ;  the stall MODE disappearing rather than getting rarer.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = Cyw43Disassoc(2000)
  Line("clean teardown before the first attempt (0 = ok)", r)
  Cyw43DrainEvents(120)

  chosen = -1
  verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_FAILED
  For i = 0 To count - 1
    If (NowMs() - startMs) > #JOIN_LADDER_BY_MS
      UartWriteStr("  LADDER STOPS ON THE CLOCK at t+")
      PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
      UartWriteStr(" ms - what is left of the")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  budget belongs to the handshake, not to another SSID.")
      PrintNl()
      Break
    EndIf
    ent = #PMKS_ADDR + #PMKS_HDR + i * #PMKS_ENTRY
    ssid = ent + #PMKS_OFF_SSID
    ssidLen = Rd32(ent + #PMKS_OFF_SLEN)

    UartWriteStr("  trying  ")
    PrintSsid(ssid, ssidLen)
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()

    r = Cyw43JoinStart(ssid, ssidLen, 2000)
    If r <> 0
      Line("    WLC_SET_SSID refused, rc", r)
      Continue
    EndIf

    slices = 0
    verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING
    While verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING And slices < #JOIN_MAX_SLICES
      verdict = Cyw43JoinPoll(#JOIN_SLICE_MS)
      If Cyw43EventNew() <> 0
        UartWriteStr("    [t+")
        PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
        UartWriteStr(" ms] event ")
        PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventType())
        UartWriteStr(" ")
        UartWriteStr(Cyw43EventName(Cyw43JoinEventType()))
        UartWriteStr(" status ")
        PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventStatus())
        UartWriteStr(" reason ")
        PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventReason())
        UartWriteStr(" flags $")
        PutHexN(Cyw43JoinEventFlags(), 4)
        UartWriteStr(" state $")
        PutHexN(Cyw43JoinState(), 4)
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()
      EndIf
      slices = slices + 1
    Wend

    If verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_JOINED
      chosen = i
      Break
    EndIf
    UartWriteStr("    FAILED: ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43JoinFailText())
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
    Cyw43Disassoc(2000)
    Cyw43DrainEvents(150)

    ; ONE RETRY, AND ONLY FOR FAILURES A RETRY CAN FIX. A stale
    ; association at the far end shows as an AUTH timeout or a keying
    ; stall and clears once we have disassociated; NONET means the
    ; name was not on the air and PRUNE means the access point does
    ; not accept what we offer, and re-asking either of those asks the
    ; identical question.
    If Cyw43JoinFail() = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_BADAUTH Or Cyw43JoinFail() = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT Or Cyw43JoinFail() = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_KEYTIMEOUT
      If (NowMs() - startMs) < #JOIN_LADDER_BY_MS
        UartWriteStr("    retrying the same network after the teardown")
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()
        r = Cyw43JoinStart(ssid, ssidLen, 2000)
        If r = 0
          slices = 0
          verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING
          While verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING And slices < #JOIN_MAX_SLICES
            verdict = Cyw43JoinPoll(#JOIN_SLICE_MS)
            If Cyw43EventNew() <> 0
              UartWriteStr("    [t+")
              PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
              UartWriteStr(" ms] event ")
              PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventType())
              UartWriteStr(" ")
              UartWriteStr(Cyw43EventName(Cyw43JoinEventType()))
              UartWriteStr(" status ")
              PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventStatus())
              UartWriteStr(" reason ")
              PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventReason())
              UartWriteStr(" flags $")
              PutHexN(Cyw43JoinEventFlags(), 4)
              UartWriteStr(" state $")
              PutHexN(Cyw43JoinState(), 4)
              PrintNl()
              UartDrain()
            EndIf
            slices = slices + 1
          Wend
          If verdict = #CYW43_JOIN_JOINED
            chosen = i
            Break
          EndIf
          UartWriteStr("    FAILED AGAIN: ")
          UartWriteStr(Cyw43JoinFailText())
          PrintNl()
          UartDrain()
          Cyw43Disassoc(2000)
          Cyw43DrainEvents(150)
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next

  If chosen < 0
    UartWriteStr("  NOTHING ASSOCIATED. There is no link to key.")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ent = #PMKS_ADDR + #PMKS_HDR + chosen * #PMKS_ENTRY
  UartWriteStr("  ASSOCIATED to  ")
  PrintSsid(ent + #PMKS_OFF_SSID, Rd32(ent + #PMKS_OFF_SLEN))
  PrintNl()
  Stamp("associated")

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 7b - WHAT THE ASSOCIATION REQUEST ACTUALLY CARRIED.
  ;
  ; ONE IOCTL, AND IT HAS TO HAPPEN HERE rather than after the
  ; handshake, because message 2 has to repeat these exact bytes.
  ; cyw43.pi4's ioctl path reuses cyw43_rx while it waits for a reply,
  ; so a data frame arriving during this call is lost - and on the
  ; 2026-08-28 bench message 1 landed 27 ms after LINK. The cost of
  ; that risk is one authenticator retry, which is why #EAPOL_M1_MS is
  ; wide enough to hold one. The cost of NOT doing it is a handshake
  ; that cannot succeed at all.
  ; ====================================================================
  theIeLen = 0
  r = Cyw43GetAssocReqIes(160, 2000)
  If r = 0
    n = Cyw43ReplyLen()
    p = Cyw43ReplyPtr()
    i = 0
    While i + 2 <= n
      j = PeekA(p + i + 1)
      If i + 2 + j > n
        Break
      EndIf
      If PeekA(p + i) = 48 And (j + 2) <= #RSN_IE_MAX
        theIeLen = j + 2
        For k = 0 To theIeLen - 1
          theIe[k] = PeekA(p + i + k)
        Next
      EndIf
      i = i + 2 + j
    Wend
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 8 - THE FOUR-WAY HANDSHAKE
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- the four-way handshake --")
  PrintNl()

  For i = 0 To 31
    thePmk[i] = PeekA(ent + #PMKS_OFF_PMK + i)
  Next
  ; apMac IS STILL ZERO HERE and that is not an oversight. The read-back
  ; that would fill it now happens after the handshake, and it is not
  ; needed: Wpa2SupHandle takes the authenticator's address from
  ; message 1's SOURCE field, which is the address that actually spoke
  ; to us. On a network where the BSSID and the transmitter differ,
  ; that is the one the PTK must be derived from.
  If theIeLen >= 2
    UartWriteStr("  the request carried:  ")
    For i = 0 To theIeLen - 1
      PutHexN(theIe[i], 2)
      UartWrite(32)
    Next
    PrintNl()
    ok = 1
    If theIeLen <> #RSN_IE_LEN
      ok = 0
    Else
      For i = 0 To #RSN_IE_LEN - 1
        If theIe[i] <> PeekA(?theRsnIe + i)
          ok = 0
        EndIf
      Next
    EndIf
    Line("  and it matches the one we asked for (want 1)", ok)
    If ok = 0
      UartWriteStr("  THE FIRMWARE CHANGED IT. Message 2 repeats what the")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  REQUEST carried, not what we asked for - the access")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  point compares against what it received.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
    ok = Wpa2SupBegin(@thePmk[0], @ourMac[0], @apMac[0], @theIe[0], theIeLen)
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  assoc_req_ies gave no RSN element - falling back to the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  constant. If message 3 never arrives, this is the first")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  thing to look at.")
    PrintNl()
    ok = Wpa2SupBegin(@thePmk[0], @ourMac[0], @apMac[0], ?theRsnIe, #RSN_IE_LEN)
  EndIf
  Line("Wpa2SupBegin (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  Wpa2SupSetSnonce(@snonce[0])
  For i = 0 To 31
    thePmk[i] = 0
  Next

  m1 = 0
  m3 = 0
  keyed = 0
  waitMs = #EAPOL_M1_MS
  t0 = NowMs()
  While (NowMs() - t0) < waitMs And m3 = 0
    n = Cyw43Receive(60)
    If n >= 14
      code = Wpa2SupHandle(Cyw43ReceivePtr(), n)
      If code = #WPA2SUP_IGNORED
        UartWriteStr("    a data frame, ")
        PrintDec(n)
        UartWriteStr(" bytes, ethertype $")
        p = Cyw43ReceivePtr()
        PutHexN(PeekA(p + 12), 2)
        PutHexN(PeekA(p + 13), 2)
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()
      ElseIf code = #WPA2SUP_SENT_M2
        m1 = m1 + 1
        UartWriteStr("    [t+")
        PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
        UartWriteStr(" ms] MESSAGE 1 IN, ")
        PrintDec(n)
        UartWriteStr(" bytes, from ")
        PrintBssid(Cyw43ReceivePtr() + 6)
        PrintNl()
        r = SendSupFrame()
        UartWriteStr("    message 2 out, ")
        PrintDec(Wpa2SupTxLen())
        UartWriteStr(" bytes, Cyw43Send rc ")
        PrintDec(r)
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()
        ; From here the AP answers in milliseconds, not seconds.
        t0 = NowMs()
        waitMs = #EAPOL_M3_MS
      ElseIf code = #WPA2SUP_SENT_M4
        m3 = 1
        UartWriteStr("    [t+")
        PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
        UartWriteStr(" ms] MESSAGE 3 IN, ")
        PrintDec(n)
        UartWriteStr(" bytes - AND ITS MIC VERIFIED")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("    *** THE ACCESS POINT HOLDS THE SAME PMK. THAT IS")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("    *** THE MUTUAL AUTHENTICATION.")
        PrintNl()
        Line("    GTK bytes    ", Wpa2SupGtkLen())
        Line("    GTK key id   ", Wpa2SupGtkIndex())
        UartDrain()

        ; ------------------------------------------------------------
        ;  MESSAGE 4 GOES OUT FIRST AND THE KEYS GO IN AFTER IT. THE
        ;  ORDER IS NOT A PREFERENCE - THE OTHER WAY ROUND CANNOT WORK.
        ;
        ;  IEEE 802.11-2016 12.7.6.5: the Authenticator installs the PTK
        ;  ON RECEPTION OF MESSAGE 4. It does not hold the pairwise key
        ;  while message 4 is in the air. Install ours first and the
        ;  radio CCMP-encrypts message 4 under a key the access point
        ;  has not plumbed, so it arrives as noise.
        ;
        ;  MEASURED, 2026-08-28. This payload installed first and the
        ;  access point retransmitted message 3 twice:
        ;
        ;      [t+5626 ms] MESSAGE 3 IN, 169 bytes - MIC VERIFIED
        ;        PTK installed (want 0)  0
        ;      message 4 out, 113 bytes, Cyw43Send rc 0
        ;      169 bytes ethertype $888E from 04:42:1A:A5:BC:90 ...
        ;      169 bytes ethertype $888E from 04:42:1A:A5:BC:90 ...
        ;
        ;  An authenticator retransmits message 3 when message 4 does
        ;  not arrive valid. IT ALSO EXPLAINS THE MISSING DHCP OFFER,
        ;  which was being chased separately: the DISCOVER went out
        ;  under a PTK the access point had never installed either.
        ;  One cause, two symptoms.
        ; ------------------------------------------------------------
        r = SendSupFrame()
        UartWriteStr("    message 4 out, ")
        PrintDec(Wpa2SupTxLen())
        UartWriteStr(" bytes, Cyw43Send rc ")
        PrintDec(r)
        UartWriteStr("   (in the clear - the AP has no PTK yet)")
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()

        r = Cyw43SetWsecKey(0, Wpa2SupTk(), Wpa2SupTkLen(), #CYW43_CRYPTO_ALGO_AES_CCM, Wpa2SupApMac(), 0, 2000)
        Line("    PTK installed (want 0)", r)
        If r <> 0
          Line("      ioctl status", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
        EndIf
        j = r
        r = Cyw43SetWsecKey(Wpa2SupGtkIndex(), Wpa2SupGtk(), Wpa2SupGtkLen(), #CYW43_CRYPTO_ALGO_AES_CCM, 0, Wpa2SupGtkRsc(), 2000)
        Line("    GTK installed (want 0)", r)
        If r <> 0
          Line("      ioctl status", Cyw43IoctlStatus())
        EndIf
        If j = 0 And r = 0
          keyed = 1
        EndIf
      Else
        UartWriteStr("    ")
        UartWriteStr(Wpa2SupErrorText(code))
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Wend

  Wpa2SupWipe()
  Cyw43KeyWipe()
  AesWipe()
  KwWipe()

  If m1 = 0
    UartWriteStr("  NO EAPOL MESSAGE 1 ARRIVED in ")
    PrintDec(#EAPOL_M1_MS)
    UartWriteStr(" ms.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  Either the association did not really complete, or the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  firmware is not passing 802.1X frames up to the host.")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  The rx data frame counter below tells those apart: a")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  non-zero count with no 802.1X means frames arrive and")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  EAPOL is being kept back; a zero count means nothing at")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  all is reaching the data path.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  If m1 > 0 And m3 = 0
    UartWriteStr("  MESSAGE 3 NEVER ARRIVED. Message 2 went out; the access")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  point did not accept it. The two things it checks are")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  the MIC - which would mean the PMK is wrong, so the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  passphrase or the SSID salt is wrong - and the RSN")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  element, which the read-back above prints in full.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  Line("keys installed (want 1)", keyed)
  Stamp("handshake over")

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 8b - THE READ-BACKS, AFTER THE HANDSHAKE AND NOT BEFORE IT.
  ;
  ; THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING AND THIS IS WHY. Message 1 arrives within
  ; tens of milliseconds of the association response, and cyw43.pi4's
  ; ioctl path reuses cyw43_rx while it waits for a reply - so a data
  ; frame that lands during WLC_GET_BSSID is overwritten and gone. Five
  ; read-back ioctls between LINK-up and the first Cyw43Receive is
  ; precisely the window in which the first EAPOL message disappears.
  ; The authenticator's one-second retry would recover it and would eat
  ; most of the listen budget doing so.
  ;
  ; The BSSID, the channel and the RSSI answer exactly the same a few
  ; hundred milliseconds later. The handshake does not get a second
  ; chance that cheaply, so it goes first.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- the link, read back from the firmware --")
  PrintNl()
  r = Cyw43ReadBssid(2000)
  Line("WLC_GET_BSSID says associated (want 1)", r)
  If r = 1
    UartWriteStr("  BSSID            ")
    PrintBssid(@cyw43_linkBssid[0])
    PrintNl()
    For j = 0 To 5
      apMac[j] = Cyw43LinkBssidByte(j)
    Next
  EndIf
  r = Cyw43ReadChannel(2000)
  If r = 0
    Line("channel          ", Cyw43LinkChannel())
    If Cyw43LinkChannel() > 14
      UartWriteStr("    that is the 5 GHz band")
    Else
      UartWriteStr("    that is the 2.4 GHz band")
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  r = Cyw43ReadRssi(2000)
  If r = 0
    Line("RSSI dBm         ", Cyw43LinkRssi())
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 7b - WHAT THE ASSOCIATION REQUEST ACTUALLY CARRIED.
  ;
  ; `wpaie` was accepted; this is the read-back that turns "accepted"
  ; into "and these are the bytes". If they differ from ours, message 2
  ; will be rejected and this row is where a reader finds out why -
  ; instead of blaming the arithmetic.
  ; ====================================================================
  ; The events that arrived AFTER the handshake, drained and printed.
  ; A DEAUTH here is the access point saying why it stopped talking,
  ; and it is the difference between "message 3 was late" and "we were
  ; thrown off the network".
  r = Cyw43DrainEvents(120)
  Line("events after the handshake", r)
  If Cyw43EventNew() <> 0
    UartWriteStr("  last event ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventType())
    UartWriteStr(" ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43EventName(Cyw43JoinEventType()))
    UartWriteStr(" status ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventStatus())
    UartWriteStr(" reason ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43JoinEventReason())
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  UartDrain()


  ; ====================================================================
  ; 9 - TRAFFIC. The proof that the keys are the RIGHT keys.
  ;
  ; Once the pairwise and group keys are in, every frame on the air is
  ; CCMP-encrypted and the firmware decrypts it before handing it up.
  ; A frame that arrives here with a sensible EtherType therefore
  ; decrypted correctly - which is a stronger statement than "the
  ; handshake completed", because a handshake can complete against a
  ; key the firmware then refuses.
  ;
  ; NOTHING IS TRANSMITTED. Any real LAN broadcasts ARP, mDNS and DHCP
  ; constantly, so listening is enough and it needs no IP address, no
  ; DHCP client and no assumption about this network's numbering. The
  ; ICMP echo that would follow needs an address, and that is the next
  ; payload's job rather than a guess made here.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- traffic --")
  PrintNl()

  frames = 0
  t0 = NowMs()
  While (NowMs() - t0) < #TRAFFIC_MS
    n = Cyw43Receive(50)
    If n >= 14
      frames = frames + 1
      If frames <= 6
        p = Cyw43ReceivePtr()
        UartWriteStr("  ")
        PrintDec(n)
        UartWriteStr(" bytes  ethertype $")
        PutHexN(PeekA(p + 12), 2)
        PutHexN(PeekA(p + 13), 2)
        UartWriteStr("  from ")
        PrintBssid(p + 6)
        UartWriteStr("  to ")
        PrintBssid(p)
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Wend
  Line("frames decrypted ", frames)
  If frames > 0
    UartWriteStr("  *** THE LINK CARRIES TRAFFIC. Those frames were CCMP on")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  *** the air and came up as valid Ethernet, which only")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  *** happens if the installed keys are the right ones.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 9b - AN ADDRESS, AND THEN A PING.
  ;
  ; THE WHOLE STAGE IS SKIPPED IF THE CLOCK HAS ALREADY GONE, and that
  ; is deliberate rather than defensive. Everything above this point is
  ; the milestone; this is the next rung. A stage that starts at
  ; t = 13 s cannot finish before the deadman fires, and a reset takes
  ; the log of the milestone with it if the drain has not kept up.
  ; #PING_START_BY_MS is the gate and it is checked once, out loud.
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- dhcp --")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  If keyed = 0
    UartWriteStr("  skipped: no keys, so nothing would be carried anyway")
    PrintNl()
  ElseIf (NowMs() - startMs) > #PING_START_BY_MS
    UartWriteStr("  SKIPPED ON THE CLOCK at t+")
    PrintDec(NowMs() - startMs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms. Starting a DHCP exchange")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  this late cannot finish inside the deadman, and a reset")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  here would cost the keying evidence above.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    ; The transaction id, from the same generator as the SNonce. It is
    ; not a secret - it is on the wire in clear inside the encrypted
    ; frame - but it must not repeat between runs, or a server's reply
    ; to the PREVIOUS boot would be accepted by this one.
    If EntropyBytes(@dhcpXid[0], 4, #ENTROPY_SPINS) <> 4
      dhcpXid[0] = ourMac[5]
      dhcpXid[1] = ourMac[4]
      dhcpXid[2] = ourMac[3]
      dhcpXid[3] = $5A
    EndIf

    n = BuildDhcp(1, 0, 0)
    r = Cyw43Send(n)
    Line("DISCOVER sent, Cyw43Send rc", r)
    UartDrain()

    got = 0
    frames = 0
    t0 = NowMs()
    While (NowMs() - t0) < #DHCP_WAIT_MS And got = 0
      n = Cyw43Receive(40)
      If n >= 14
        If ParseDhcp(Cyw43ReceivePtr(), n) = 2
          got = 2
          For j = 0 To 5
            srvMac[j] = PeekA(Cyw43ReceivePtr() + 6 + j)
          Next
          srvMacOk = 1
        Else
          ; EVERY OTHER FRAME IS PRINTED, and that is the whole
          ; correction. The previous run received three data frames
          ; and reported zero twice, because anything this loop did
          ; not recognise it dropped in silence. A frame arriving at
          ; all is the proof the keys work; only its CONTENT is a DHCP
          ; question.
          frames = frames + 1
          If frames <= 6
            p = Cyw43ReceivePtr()
            UartWriteStr("    ")
            PrintDec(n)
            UartWriteStr(" bytes  ethertype $")
            PutHexN(PeekA(p + 12), 2)
            PutHexN(PeekA(p + 13), 2)
            UartWriteStr("  from ")
            PrintBssid(p + 6)
            UartWriteStr("  to ")
            PrintBssid(p)
            PrintNl()
            UartDrain()
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    Wend
    If frames > 0
      Line("frames arrived while waiting", frames)
      UartWriteStr("  *** THOSE CAME OFF THE AIR CCMP-ENCRYPTED AND DECODED")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  *** AS VALID ETHERNET. THE LINK CARRIES TRAFFIC.")
      PrintNl()
      UartDrain()
    EndIf

    If got <> 2
      UartWriteStr("  NO OFFER in ")
      PrintDec(#DHCP_WAIT_MS)
      UartWriteStr(" ms. The frames above show the link decrypts,")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  so this is a DHCP question rather than a radio one.")
      PrintNl()
    Else
      UartWriteStr("  OFFER      ")
      PrintIp(dhcpIp)
      UartWriteStr("  mask ")
      PrintIp(dhcpMask)
      UartWriteStr("  router ")
      PrintIp(dhcpGw)
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  *** THAT DATAGRAM CAME BACK CCMP-ENCRYPTED AND")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  *** DECRYPTED CORRECTLY. The link carries IP.")
      PrintNl()
      UartDrain()

      n = BuildDhcp(3, dhcpIp, dhcpSrv)
      r = Cyw43Send(n)
      Line("REQUEST sent, Cyw43Send rc", r)
      UartDrain()

      got = 0
      t0 = NowMs()
      While (NowMs() - t0) < #DHCP_ACK_MS And got = 0
        n = Cyw43Receive(40)
        If n >= 14
          j = ParseDhcp(Cyw43ReceivePtr(), n)
          If j = 5
            got = 5
            For k = 0 To 5
              srvMac[k] = PeekA(Cyw43ReceivePtr() + 6 + k)
            Next
            srvMacOk = 1
          ElseIf j = 6
            got = 6
          EndIf
        EndIf
      Wend

      If got = 6
        UartWriteStr("  the server sent a NAK - it will not lease that address")
        PrintNl()
      ElseIf got <> 5
        UartWriteStr("  no ACK, but the OFFER above already proved the link")
        PrintNl()
      Else
        UartWriteStr("  ACK        ")
        PrintIp(dhcpIp)
        PrintNl()
        Stamp("addressed")

        ; --------------------------------------------------------
        ;  DOES THIS NETWORK OFFER A FILE SERVER?
        ;
        ;  The last rung of the standing request is a file transfer,
        ;  and tftp.pi4 is a complete client waiting for an address.
        ;  There is no `net.server` in SETTINGS.TXT - the eight keys
        ;  it holds are four SSIDs and four passphrases - so the only
        ;  honest source is the network itself. Asked, and whatever
        ;  comes back is printed. NOTHING IS INVENTED: a guessed
        ;  address on somebody's home LAN sends packets to a stranger.
        ; --------------------------------------------------------
        UartWriteStr("  next server (siaddr)  ")
        PrintIp(dhcpNext)
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("  option 150 tftp addr  ")
        PrintIp(dhcpTftpIp)
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("  option  66 tftp name  ")
        If dhcpTftpNameLen > 0
          PrintSsid(@dhcpTftpName[0], dhcpTftpNameLen)
        Else
          UartWriteStr("<none offered>")
        EndIf
        PrintNl()
        If dhcpNext = 0 And dhcpTftpIp = 0 And dhcpTftpNameLen = 0
          UartWriteStr("  THIS NETWORK ADVERTISES NO FILE SERVER. The transfer")
          PrintNl()
          UartWriteStr("  rung needs one address and one filename; tftp.pi4 is")
          PrintNl()
          UartWriteStr("  ready for both. Put them in SETTINGS.TXT as")
          PrintNl()
          UartWriteStr("  net.server, or run a TFTP server and say where.")
          PrintNl()
        EndIf
        UartDrain()

        ; ------------------------------------------------------------
        ; 9c - THE PING.
        ; ------------------------------------------------------------
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("-- ping --")
        PrintNl()
        UartDrain()

        NetInit()
        NetSetMac(#HW_LINK_WIFI, @ourMac[0])
        ok = NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIFI, dhcpIp, dhcpMask, dhcpGw)
        Line("NetSetIPv4 (want 1)", ok)
        If ok = 0
          UartWriteStr("  ")
          UartWriteStr(NetErrorText())
          PrintNl()
        Else
          ; ----------------------------------------------------------
          ;  SEED THE CACHE FROM THE DHCP REPLY WE ALREADY HAVE.
          ;
          ;  The ACK arrived as an Ethernet frame from the server, so
          ;  its source address IS the server's hardware address. Only
          ;  the address the frame actually came from is seeded - where
          ;  the DHCP server and the default gateway are different
          ;  machines, the gateway is still ARPed for, because putting
          ;  the wrong MAC against the right IP fails as "the ping went
          ;  nowhere" and reads as a network fault.
          ; ----------------------------------------------------------
          If srvMacOk = 1
            UartWriteStr("  the DHCP server answered from  ")
            PrintBssid(@srvMac[0])
            UartWriteStr("  (")
            PrintIp(dhcpSrv)
            UartWriteStr(")")
            PrintNl()
            NetArpSet(#HW_LINK_WIFI, dhcpSrv, @srvMac[0])
            If dhcpSrv = dhcpGw
              UartWriteStr("  it is also the router, so the cache is seeded and")
              PrintNl()
              UartWriteStr("  no ARP round trip is needed")
              PrintNl()
            EndIf
          EndIf
          UartDrain()

          ; The router has to be resolved before anything can be sent
          ; to it. NetArpRequest stages the request; the reply comes
          ; back through NetInput as #NET_IN_LEARNED.
          If NetArpLookup(#HW_LINK_WIFI, dhcpGw, @peerMac[0]) = 0
            r = NetArpRequest(#HW_LINK_WIFI, dhcpGw)
            Line("  NetArpRequest (want 1)", r)
            If r = 1
              r = SendNetOut()
              Line("  ARP request sent, Cyw43Send rc", r)
            Else
              UartWriteStr("  ")
              UartWriteStr(NetErrorText())
              PrintNl()
            EndIf
            UartDrain()
            t0 = NowMs()
            While (NowMs() - t0) < #ARP_WAIT_MS
              n = Cyw43Receive(30)
              If n >= 14
                code = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIFI, Cyw43ReceivePtr(), n)
                If code = #NET_IN_REPLY
                  SendNetOut()
                EndIf
              EndIf
              If NetArpLookup(#HW_LINK_WIFI, dhcpGw, @peerMac[0]) = 1
                Break
              EndIf
            Wend
          EndIf
          ok = NetArpLookup(#HW_LINK_WIFI, dhcpGw, @peerMac[0])
          Line("router resolved (want 1)", ok)
          If ok = 1
            UartWriteStr("  router MAC       ")
            PrintBssid(@peerMac[0])
            PrintNl()
            UartDrain()

            got = 0
            For i = 1 To #PING_COUNT
              If NetPingBuild(#HW_LINK_WIFI, dhcpGw, $4321, i, 32) = 0
                UartWriteStr("  ")
                UartWriteStr(NetErrorText())
                PrintNl()
                Break
              EndIf
              SendNetOut()
              t0 = NowMs()
              While (NowMs() - t0) < #PING_WAIT_MS
                n = Cyw43Receive(30)
                If n >= 14
                  code = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIFI, Cyw43ReceivePtr(), n)
                  If code = #NET_IN_REPLY
                    SendNetOut()
                  ElseIf code = #NET_IN_PONG
                    got = got + 1
                    UartWriteStr("  echo reply seq ")
                    PrintDec(NetPongSeq())
                    UartWriteStr(" from ")
                    PrintIp(NetPongFrom())
                    UartWriteStr(", ")
                    PrintDec(NetPongBytes())
                    UartWriteStr(" bytes")
                    PrintNl()
                    UartDrain()
                    Break
                  EndIf
                EndIf
              Wend
            Next
            Line("echo replies     ", got)
            If got > 0
              UartWriteStr("  *** PING. The Pi 4 is on the network.")
              PrintNl()
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  UartDrain()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 10 - the counters
  ; ====================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- transport counters --")
  PrintNl()
  Line("rx frames        ", Cyw43RxFrames())
  Line("rx data frames   ", Cyw43RxDataFrames())
  Line("tx data frames   ", Cyw43TxFrames())
  Line("bad headers      ", Cyw43RxBadHdr())
  Line("too short        ", Cyw43RxShort())
  Line("other channels   ", Cyw43RxOther())
  Line("other events     ", Cyw43EvOther())
  Line("empty events     ", Cyw43EvEmpty())
  Line("credit stalls    ", Cyw43Stalls())
  Line("icv errors       ", Cyw43IcvErrors())
  PrintNl()

  Stamp("done")
  If keyed = 1
    UartWriteStr("=== KEYED ===")
  Else
    UartWriteStr("=== not keyed ===")
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Main()
